On Windows, `moduleGraph` keys are backslash-separated paths produced by the builder (e.g. `C:\project\src\Button.tsx`), while `normalizedPath` uses forward slashes. As a result, `moduleGraph.get(normalizedPath)` will **always** miss on Windows — the direct O(1) fast-path effectively becomes dead code on the platform this PR targets. Every call to `findAffectedStoryFiles` will fall through to the O(n) linear scan on lines 14–18, iterating over the entire module graph and calling `normalizePath` for each entry.

With a large project (thousands of modules) and multiple changed files, this is repeated O(n·m) work. A more efficient approach is to build a normalized lookup map once before the BFS starts and pass it through:

```typescript
const normalizedModuleGraph = new Map<string, Set<ModuleNode>>();
for (const [k, v] of moduleGraph) {
  normalizedModuleGraph.set(normalizePath(k), v);
}
```

This makes the lookup O(1) unconditionally, and eliminates the need for both `getModuleNodesByNormalizedPath`'s fallback loop and the per-step `normalizePath(importer.file)` call inside the BFS traversal.
